WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:00.967
Senate Minority

00:00:00.967 --> 00:00:01.501
Leader, the

00:00:01.501 --> 00:00:02.102
Republican, Mitch

00:00:02.102 --> 00:00:02.702
McConnell,

00:00:02.702 --> 00:00:05.572
says he is fine after a truly

00:00:05.872 --> 00:00:08.108
unnerving movement on Capitol Hill.

00:00:08.141 --> 00:00:08.708
On camera

00:00:08.708 --> 00:00:09.342
this afternoon,

00:00:09.342 --> 00:00:10.276
McConnell

00:00:10.276 --> 00:00:11.878
stopped speaking

00:00:11.878 --> 00:00:13.179
for some 30 seconds

00:00:13.179 --> 00:00:14.314
right in the middle of remarks

00:00:14.314 --> 00:00:15.749
at his regularly scheduled

00:00:15.749 --> 00:00:17.217
news conference.

00:00:17.217 --> 00:00:18.752
Here is that moment

00:00:20.286 --> 00:00:21.388
After finishing

00:00:21.388 --> 00:00:23.456
the NDAA,

00:00:24.591 --> 00:00:25.725
this week has been

00:00:25.725 --> 00:00:28.862
good bipartisan cooperation

00:00:28.862 --> 00:00:31.297
and a string of

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:48.481
stuff that

00:00:49.049 --> 00:00:52.252
Senator Clinton said during the show.

00:00:53.253 --> 00:00:55.355
To make this change.

00:00:55.455 --> 00:00:56.356
Anything else you want to say?

00:00:56.356 --> 00:00:57.957
I'm sure to go back to

00:00:58.158 --> 00:00:59.192
do you want to say anything else

00:00:59.192 --> 00:01:00.260
to the press?

00:01:03.296 --> 00:01:04.497
John Dickerson,

00:01:05.432 --> 00:01:06.332
let's go back to you.

00:01:06.332 --> 00:01:07.500
Go ahead, John.

00:01:08.968 --> 00:01:10.470
Quite a concerning moment there.

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:12.405
CNN's Michael Rogers on Capitol Hill.

00:01:12.405 --> 00:01:14.040
He was there as it was happening.

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:14.808
And want to understand.

00:01:14.808 --> 00:01:16.743
You spoke to the senator

00:01:16.743 --> 00:01:19.045
himself afterwards.

00:01:19.045 --> 00:01:19.813
What did he say to you?

00:01:21.247 --> 00:01:21.481
Yeah,

00:01:21.481 --> 00:01:22.082
he didn't really

00:01:22.082 --> 00:01:24.284
get into the details about what happened.

00:01:24.517 --> 00:01:26.152
I tried to ask him about that

00:01:26.152 --> 00:01:27.620
very unnerving moment

00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:29.222
that alarmed his colleagues

00:01:29.222 --> 00:01:30.723
that sent Senator McConnell

00:01:30.723 --> 00:01:33.226
away from this weekly press conference.

00:01:33.226 --> 00:01:34.661
He walked over to his office,

00:01:34.661 --> 00:01:35.829
which is a short walk

00:01:35.829 --> 00:01:37.297
from where that press conference was.

00:01:37.297 --> 00:01:38.598
And then he later returned

00:01:38.598 --> 00:01:40.500
and answered questions from reporters

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:40.934
like me

00:01:40.934 --> 00:01:42.302
when I asked him about this

00:01:42.302 --> 00:01:43.236
and whether he can continue

00:01:43.236 --> 00:01:44.137
to do his job.

00:01:44.137 --> 00:01:47.140
He indicated he could

00:01:48.274 --> 00:01:49.442
Could you address what happened here

00:01:49.442 --> 00:01:51.077
at the start of the press conference

00:01:51.077 --> 00:01:52.846
and it was related to your

00:01:52.846 --> 00:01:54.013
injury from earlier this year

00:01:54.013 --> 00:01:55.215
where you suffered a concussion.

00:01:55.215 --> 00:01:57.283
Is that going to

00:01:58.151 --> 00:01:59.919
define your fully able to, you know,

00:01:59.919 --> 00:02:02.455
do your job, you know, later tonight

00:02:05.191 --> 00:02:06.893
Now, McConnell aide

00:02:06.893 --> 00:02:07.260
told me

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:08.428
that the leader

00:02:08.428 --> 00:02:10.630
at that moment felt lightheaded.

00:02:10.864 --> 00:02:11.865
And then he came back

00:02:11.865 --> 00:02:12.999
and he answered questions over.

00:02:12.999 --> 00:02:14.200
It's about five questions

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:14.734
or so

00:02:14.734 --> 00:02:15.969
on a range of issues, ranging

00:02:15.969 --> 00:02:17.570
from impeachment to the Hunter Biden

00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:18.838
plea deal that collapsed.

00:02:18.838 --> 00:02:20.306
But there have been a number of questions

00:02:20.306 --> 00:02:21.474
about McConnell's health

00:02:21.474 --> 00:02:22.742
since earlier this year

00:02:22.742 --> 00:02:24.177
when at the age of 81

00:02:24.177 --> 00:02:26.346
he fell and hit his head

00:02:26.346 --> 00:02:28.314
at a Washington hotel during an event.

00:02:28.314 --> 00:02:30.316
He later suffered a concussion

00:02:30.316 --> 00:02:31.551
as well as

00:02:31.618 --> 00:02:34.254
sustained fractures to his ribs.

00:02:34.254 --> 00:02:36.356
He when he went to the hospital

00:02:36.356 --> 00:02:37.490
for some time,

00:02:37.490 --> 00:02:38.725
he was out of the Senate

00:02:38.725 --> 00:02:40.793
for several weeks later returned.

00:02:41.161 --> 00:02:42.162
He has been walking,

00:02:42.162 --> 00:02:42.762
tends to walk

00:02:42.762 --> 00:02:45.031
gingerly it's been that way for some time

00:02:45.031 --> 00:02:46.733
because he suffered polio as a kid.

00:02:46.733 --> 00:02:48.401
He walks with a slight limp.

00:02:48.401 --> 00:02:49.202
But in recent years,

00:02:49.202 --> 00:02:51.337
he has had incidents where he has fallen.

00:02:51.337 --> 00:02:53.306
And the most recent one, of course,

00:02:53.306 --> 00:02:54.407
causing a lot of concerns

00:02:54.407 --> 00:02:55.141
about his health.

00:02:55.141 --> 00:02:56.643
Now, there was another situation

00:02:56.643 --> 00:02:57.610
a couple of months ago

00:02:57.610 --> 00:02:59.879
when at the same weekly press conference

00:03:00.046 --> 00:03:01.147
that he has,

00:03:01.147 --> 00:03:03.550
he couldn't hear reporters questions

00:03:03.883 --> 00:03:05.251
who were clearly audible.

00:03:05.251 --> 00:03:06.419
The questions were very audible.

00:03:06.419 --> 00:03:07.320
They were nearby.

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:09.355
He had a difficult time hearing.

00:03:09.355 --> 00:03:11.191
And in another situation

00:03:11.191 --> 00:03:12.058
that's raising questions

00:03:12.058 --> 00:03:13.059
about his health.

00:03:13.059 --> 00:03:13.593
Now, McConnell

00:03:13.593 --> 00:03:15.662
indicating that he's not going anywhere,

00:03:15.662 --> 00:03:17.096
that he plans to serve out

00:03:17.096 --> 00:03:17.997
at least

00:03:17.997 --> 00:03:19.365
through the end of this Congress.

00:03:19.365 --> 00:03:21.034
He's not up for reelection again

00:03:21.034 --> 00:03:22.502
until 20, 26

00:03:22.502 --> 00:03:24.037
he has not made any announcements

00:03:24.037 --> 00:03:24.871
about whether you try

00:03:24.871 --> 00:03:25.838
to become leader again

00:03:25.838 --> 00:03:26.940
in the next Congress

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:28.575
or whether he'd run for reelection again.

00:03:28.575 --> 00:03:30.043
But this will only add to questions

00:03:30.043 --> 00:03:31.110
about his future

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:33.079
in this very unnerving moment.

00:03:33.079 --> 00:03:33.446
Jim.

00:03:33.613 --> 00:03:35.481
Manu Raju on the Hill, thanks so much.

00:03:35.481 --> 00:03:36.716
So let's put some of those questions

00:03:36.716 --> 00:03:37.083
to Dr.

00:03:37.083 --> 00:03:38.351
Sanjay Gupta.

00:03:38.351 --> 00:03:38.651
Dr.

00:03:38.651 --> 00:03:41.854
Gupta, I always hesitate to ask you

00:03:41.854 --> 00:03:43.890
to diagnose from afar,

00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:45.391
but this is your field.

00:03:45.391 --> 00:03:47.927
And I wonder, as you saw that

00:03:47.927 --> 00:03:48.861
really heart wrenching.

00:03:48.861 --> 00:03:51.764
30 some seconds there of that pause,

00:03:51.898 --> 00:03:52.765
freezing up.

00:03:52.765 --> 00:03:54.901
What struck you as significant

00:03:57.270 --> 00:03:58.805
Well, that was concerning.

00:03:58.805 --> 00:04:00.240
There's no no question about it.

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:00.607
And I

00:04:00.607 --> 00:04:01.140
and I think

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:04.110
he's going to need a thorough examination

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:04.877
and workup.

00:04:04.877 --> 00:04:05.979
And I'm sure his doctors

00:04:05.979 --> 00:04:07.981
will probably look at that clip

00:04:08.414 --> 00:04:09.849
just to sort of

00:04:10.083 --> 00:04:10.283
try and

00:04:10.283 --> 00:04:12.218
figure out what exactly happened there.

00:04:12.218 --> 00:04:13.019
I think, you know,

00:04:13.019 --> 00:04:14.921
I mean, there's all sorts of things

00:04:14.921 --> 00:04:15.989
that that come to mind.

00:04:15.989 --> 00:04:18.124
But as you correctly point out, Jim,

00:04:18.124 --> 00:04:19.792
some of those things will will

00:04:19.792 --> 00:04:20.994
become clearer

00:04:20.994 --> 00:04:23.162
once he gets a more thorough examination.

00:04:23.363 --> 00:04:24.831
Common things being common.

00:04:24.831 --> 00:04:25.798
You have to wonder

00:04:25.798 --> 00:04:28.201
if someone is dehydrated.

00:04:28.201 --> 00:04:28.901
Did they?

00:04:28.901 --> 00:04:30.770
Is this some kind of medication

00:04:30.770 --> 00:04:31.938
side effect?

00:04:31.938 --> 00:04:32.972
But also, you know,

00:04:32.972 --> 00:04:33.873
more seriously,

00:04:33.873 --> 00:04:36.142
was this a petite mal seizure?

00:04:36.209 --> 00:04:37.110
A mini seizure,

00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:37.677
so to speak,

00:04:37.677 --> 00:04:40.380
or even a transient ischemic attack?

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:41.848
A sort of mini stroke?

00:04:41.848 --> 00:04:43.950
Again, that's speculation.

00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:46.185
And, you know, but but it's interesting.

00:04:46.185 --> 00:04:47.220
Manu asked that question.

00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:49.289
I was just looking at the clock

00:04:49.289 --> 00:04:50.790
for about 23 seconds.

00:04:50.790 --> 00:04:52.558
He was standing at the lectern

00:04:52.558 --> 00:04:54.327
before someone asked if he was okay.

00:04:54.327 --> 00:04:56.195
And then it was 32 seconds

00:04:56.195 --> 00:04:57.730
when he sort of walked off.

00:04:57.730 --> 00:04:58.631
When he was walking off,

00:04:58.631 --> 00:05:00.199
he was moving his arms and his legs.

00:05:00.199 --> 00:05:00.566
Okay.

00:05:00.566 --> 00:05:02.302
So that was a good sign

00:05:02.302 --> 00:05:03.303
that what had happened

00:05:03.303 --> 00:05:05.438
seemed like it had started to resolve

00:05:05.772 --> 00:05:07.073
and then I think it was about 12 minutes

00:05:07.073 --> 00:05:09.542
later when Manu asked him that question.

00:05:09.542 --> 00:05:11.844
And he sort of said he was doing okay.

00:05:12.211 --> 00:05:14.414
But clearly something happened there.

00:05:14.414 --> 00:05:15.481
You have to

00:05:15.481 --> 00:05:16.582
you have to assume common

00:05:16.582 --> 00:05:18.251
things are common, first of all.

00:05:18.251 --> 00:05:20.620
But you do have to also rule out

00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:22.155
potentially more serious things.

00:05:22.155 --> 00:05:24.257
And he needs an exam. He needs.

00:05:24.257 --> 00:05:25.024
He says he's fine.

00:05:25.024 --> 00:05:26.926
But he needs to get checked out clearly.

00:05:26.926 --> 00:05:28.261
I asked Monitor

00:05:28.261 --> 00:05:29.896
that if he got an immediate exam.

00:05:29.896 --> 00:05:31.264
To his knowledge, he did not.

00:05:31.264 --> 00:05:32.732
I think when I'm thinking of older

00:05:32.732 --> 00:05:33.533
relatives, for instance,

00:05:33.533 --> 00:05:36.369
see the acronym right. Be fast, right.

00:05:36.402 --> 00:05:37.570
You look at various symptoms.

00:05:37.570 --> 00:05:39.672
If, for instance, that were a stroke.

00:05:40.173 --> 00:05:43.242
Balance eye movement face, droopy bit.

00:05:43.242 --> 00:05:44.210
But a key part of it.

00:05:44.210 --> 00:05:46.913
Right, is is quick a quick assessment.

00:05:47.613 --> 00:05:49.315
If a relative of yours,

00:05:49.315 --> 00:05:50.683
say, or a patient of yours

00:05:50.683 --> 00:05:52.352
exhibited such symptoms,

00:05:52.352 --> 00:05:54.320
you would recommend that they see

00:05:54.320 --> 00:05:55.254
they seek medical care

00:05:55.254 --> 00:05:56.522
or at least an assessment right away.

00:05:58.157 --> 00:05:59.058
Absolutely.

00:05:59.058 --> 00:06:01.561
I mean, time is is critically important.

00:06:01.861 --> 00:06:03.863
Again, I don't want to overstate this,

00:06:03.863 --> 00:06:04.097
you know,

00:06:04.097 --> 00:06:05.131
and be careful in terms

00:06:05.131 --> 00:06:06.165
of how we're presenting this.

00:06:06.165 --> 00:06:07.700
But but I think that

00:06:07.700 --> 00:06:08.468
there was clearly

00:06:08.468 --> 00:06:10.970
some sort of neurological event

00:06:10.970 --> 00:06:11.771
that happened there,

00:06:11.771 --> 00:06:13.172
whether it was just

00:06:13.172 --> 00:06:13.740
because of

00:06:13.740 --> 00:06:15.641
dehydration, or medication

00:06:15.641 --> 00:06:17.877
or something like that, perhaps.

00:06:17.877 --> 00:06:18.678
But that was, again.

00:06:18.678 --> 00:06:20.813
23 seconds before he was asked

00:06:20.813 --> 00:06:21.547
if he was okay.

00:06:21.547 --> 00:06:24.417
32 seconds before he left the lectern

00:06:24.751 --> 00:06:26.219
they watched closely.

00:06:26.219 --> 00:06:28.488
Again, he was doing it of his own power,

00:06:28.488 --> 00:06:29.622
you know, so

00:06:29.622 --> 00:06:30.923
he talked about that acronym.

00:06:30.923 --> 00:06:32.225
One of the things they're testing for

00:06:32.225 --> 00:06:33.259
is strength.

00:06:33.259 --> 00:06:34.627
And it appeared that he had strength on

00:06:34.627 --> 00:06:36.195
both the right and left side of his body.

00:06:36.195 --> 00:06:40.299
But but a mini seizure, a mini stroke,

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:42.969
or again, something more common,

00:06:42.969 --> 00:06:46.139
whatever it is that needs to be assessed.

00:06:46.172 --> 00:06:47.073
So

00:06:47.373 --> 00:06:49.442
I'm hoping that his staff is getting him

00:06:49.709 --> 00:06:51.244
to the hospital to get that checked out.

